<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> a heated jacket is worth it if you run cold, ski or snowshoe in very cold weather, or spend a lot of time standing still. Choose one that's warm and weather-resistant with the heat off, puts heat on your core (chest and back), has several heat settings, and has a battery that fits comfortably in its pocket. Plan your layers so the jacket works even when the battery is dead.</p></div>

<h2>Who heated jackets are for</h2>
<p>A good layering system keeps most people warm on the mountain. Heated jackets make the biggest difference for:</p>
<ul>
  <li>People who get cold easily, even in good layers.</li>
  <li>Skiers and riders in very cold regions, or anyone on long, exposed chairlifts.</li>
  <li>People who stop a lot: parents with kids in ski school, spectators, photographers, and winter hikers taking breaks.</li>
  <li>Around-town wear on cold mornings, commutes, and outdoor events.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you're only occasionally cold, start with a better <%= link_to "base layer", guide_path("best-base-layer-for-skiing") %> and mid layer. See <%= link_to "what to wear skiing", guide_path("what-to-wear-skiing") %> for the full layering system.</p>

<h2>How heated jackets work</h2>
<p>Thin, flexible heating elements are sewn into panels of the jacket, usually across the chest, upper back, and sometimes the collar or hand pockets. A rechargeable battery pack, kept in an interior pocket, powers them. A button, often on the chest, switches between heat levels.</p>
<p>Heated pieces come in a few styles:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Heated vests:</strong> core heat without sleeves, easy to wear as a mid layer under a ski shell.</li>
  <li><strong>Heated mid layers:</strong> fleece or light insulated jackets meant to go under a waterproof shell.</li>
  <li><strong>Heated outer jackets:</strong> insulated jackets meant to be worn on their own in cold, dry weather. Check whether they're actually waterproof before skiing in them.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What to look for</h2>
<h3>Warm without power</h3>
<p>This matters most. Batteries run down, and you'll spend part of the day with the heat low or off. The jacket should still be a useful insulating layer on its own.</p>
<h3>Where the heat goes</h3>
<p>Core heat (chest and back) does the most good, because a warm core helps keep your hands and feet warmer too. Collar and pocket zones are nice extras.</p>
<h3>Heat settings and battery life</h3>
<p>Run time depends on the heat setting, the outside temperature, and how many zones are on. High heat drains the battery fastest. Look at the run time the maker lists for each setting, plan to use low or medium most of the day, and consider a spare battery for long days.</p>
<h3>Battery size and pocket placement</h3>
<p>A battery adds weight. Make sure the battery pocket sits where it won't bump your hip belt, harness, or ski pack, and that the cable is easy to reach.</p>
<h3>Fit for layering</h3>
<p>Heating elements work best close to your body. A heated vest or mid layer should fit snugly under your shell without being tight. Try it with the layers you'll actually wear.</p>
<h3>Washing</h3>
<p>Most heated garments can be washed once the battery is removed, but follow the care label exactly. Some are hand-wash only or need a gentle cycle and air drying.</p>
<h3>Charging and travel</h3>
<p>Check how the battery charges and how long it takes. If you fly, lithium battery rules apply. Airlines generally require spare lithium batteries to travel in carry-on bags, not checked luggage, and set limits on battery size. Check your airline's current rules before you pack, and see the <%= link_to "ski trip packing list", guide_path("ski-trip-packing-list") %> for everything else.</p>

